Agent to Tools: MCP
--------------


What happens when your AI agent needs another agent it has never met, built by a different company, to finish the job?

They need a shared language. That is A2A.

Remember MCP from earlier? It connects an agent to its tools. A2A goes the other direction. It connects an agent to other agents.

The idea is simple. Every agent publishes an Agent Card, a small public profile that says what it can do and how to reach it. Your agent finds another agent, reads its card, and hands off a task. Different framework, different cloud, different company, does not matter. Neither side exposes its internals. They just exchange the task and the result.

Picture a travel agent you built. To close a booking, it delegates flights to one vendor's agent and hotels to another's. Different teams, different stacks. They coordinate anyway, because they all speak A2A.

The future is not one mega-agent. It is many specialists, owned by different people, working together. A2A is how they talk without custom glue for every pair.

Built by Google, now an open standard under the Linux Foundation, with Java in the official SDKs. Your agents can join the conversation.



